i felt worse he said then a moment later  no i haven't but i'm all right how bad  was it you're both dead naomi said  seriously we had to override the triage  filters on both of you more than once  the expert system kept clicking you over  into hospice care and shooting you full  of morphine hey guys pete here in just  over two months the final book in the  expanse series will finally be here and  i'm pretty excited in just over a decade  they've delivered a nine volume tail and  i can't wait to see how it ends to get  ready i'm doing a re-read of the entire  series from the beginning and today i'm  going to talk about why you should read  the books if you haven't done that yet  and some of what you'll get out of it if  you've only watched the show i'm gonna  do that by highlighting the most  interesting changes from book to show  that stood out to me while re-reading  leviathan wakes and since the story gets  to pretty much the same place there's  not really any spoilers here if you've  seen the first couple of seasons  if you're here you've probably watched  my videos about the tv series and you  already know that i encourage everyone  that likes that to read the books i get  asked that question all the time in the  comments of my videos and just to  reiterate you should definitely read the  books if you like these characters if  you like them even a little bit then  you'll get a whole different take on  them when you experience the story  through their points of view a lot of  people ask if you can just skip right to  book six if you want to find out how the  tv show might end and i think that would  be a mistake technically you could get  away with that but you would be confused  by some of the characters and you'll  likely end up going back and starting  from the beginning at some point anyway  it's a situation where the authors of  the book are producers on the show and  they're in the writers room guiding the  story and contributing to the writing of  the episodes which means it is a close  adaptation but it's different enough  that it doesn't feel redundant so  ideally start at the beginning and just  read everything in publication order  including the novellas if you can  because those are awesome and they add a  lot to the main books even though they  are quick reads there isn't a lot of  time left so you might not be able to  make it through all of them by the time  leviathan falls comes out but you'd  likely be able to get caught up with the  show by the time the final season comes  out and then you'd be ready to finish  the story off by reading the final  trilogy i would also say that the audio  books are highly recommended a lot of  times i prefer listening to audiobooks  after i know who the main characters are  and what's going on in a series so  having seen the show makes that really  easy here on top of that jefferson maze  does a fantastic job and his performance  really adds to the experience it's  actually become a big part of the whole  thing for me at this point and i can't  even hold the fact against him that he  had me saying abasarala's name wrong  when i started making videos  as to the why the honest number one  selling point i have for reading the  expanse books is that they're a lot of  fun maybe that seems uninspired or  reductive but it really is my favorite  thing about reading them because they do  a great job of developing the characters  i really enjoy seeing how they react to  the high-stakes situations that they're  always finding themselves in the second  anna leviathan wakes specific selling  point is the character miller you get to  know him so much better in this book and  since his whole art concludes by the end  of it this really is a must read if  you're a miller fan who he is and how he  sees things really does help to flesh  out this world he's a belter he works  for an earth corporation his partner's  an earther there's a lot of extra  details you pick up seeing things  through his eyes and having access to  his thoughts  beyond the fun and the characters the  fact that the imagined near future  setting is believable is a big plus and  while the authors seem to go to great  lengths to try to get things right  scientifically it's never overwhelming  most of the world building happens hand  in hand with the character development  which means you can still follow what's  going on even if you're not absorbing  all the finer points about how  everything works in space  for me i end up caring about the  characters nothing about this time that  they're in seems completely unrealistic  i can imagine it it feels lived in and  the culture and attitudes track with how  the world is now and i know their  tendency towards hard sci-fi is a big  selling point for some i'm okay with it  feeling believable to my non-scientist  mind but there's a lot more that's  spelled out in the books if you're  really trying to wrap your head around  the feasibility of it all compared to  this show the best thing about leviathan  wakes is that there are only two primary  points of view you learn about working  to make a living to get by in the harsh  environment of space and the future crew  of the ross ante through holden's  thoughts and you learn about life in the  belt working on a station that's  controlled by the corporate interests of  the inner planets from millers there's  no earth subplot with abasarala and  aaronright in the first book which means  the focus stays on protogen's actions  leading up to the eros incident and how  these two different characters with  different backgrounds and perspectives  react and how their stories get drawn  together  overall that works a lot better for  setting the table and i think the world  building is more natural and easy to  pick up on i came to the books through  the show i watched it first so i already  knew how things were gonna play out more  or less but in that first season there  was so much going on so many characters  to meet and remember and so many  different locations it was easy to miss  a lot of those world building details  since there's less to keep track of the  book does a better job setting up the  series and what it's about you can see  how colonization is working out for the  belters who wound up being the meat for  the machine it's clear that it's a story  about people and how they're affected by  the various events that take place in  the series including those that are of  extra solar origin the baseline is  exploitation and there's a lot of  potential for things to get worse  and that's a great place to transition  to talking about the changes from book  to show because the changes in the  belters bodies is one of the big things  that the show couldn't pull off in the  beginning of season one they show you a  little bit of what it would look like  but then it's just really not practical  and they start to rely on things like  the way that they dress and their  tattoos to kind of set them apart but a  big part of this whole story is that  these are humans that went out and lived  in low gravity which caused their bodies  to change in a way that you can see that  makes them easy to otherwise to be  thought of as less than human and  historically we know how that turns out  one of the first surprises i had reading  the book was that the beginning is a lot  different for one holden's already the  exo and the future crew of the rossi  know each other already they already get  along once the can is destroyed they  naturally think of holden as the leader  and there's a lot less pushback there's  less conflict altogether the knight is  never damaged because that mostly served  to show how difficult it can get when  things go wrong in space the book can  use holden's internal dialogue to flesh  out the characters and those same  details so you don't have to have that  situation to establish things there are  trade-offs but i mostly prefer the  book's version in most cases just to be  clear though it's a matter of adaptation  from one medium to the other most of the  changes that they made make sense in  that context it's all a way of getting  to the same place relying on what works  for the different mediums i do feel like  they developed alex and amos earlier in  the show and i did like the way that  they handled the doninger a little  better on tv captain yao was a good  addition and the foreshadowing of  naomi's secret past that won't be  revealed until season five is pretty  effective i like that in the book there  was no add a scene that played like a  mystery about what she was gonna say but  it never really goes anywhere in my mind  things do develop better without the  crew drama and by seeing everyone how  holden sees them there's less guessing  about where things are going there's a  mutual respect and a bond that's forged  through the experiences on the cant and  donager  one thing that jumped out to me the  first time i read the book is that  holden is the one that gives fred the  proto-molecule sample he just does it  without much fanfare when he's trying to  buy more time from miller who came up  with the idea of trying to convince  julie not to crash eros into earth the  show deciding to have naomi do that  later on is supposed to show her  complicated feelings as a belter but i  think it just came off as a betrayal to  a lot of viewers and diminished her  character i also thought the  relationship between holden and naomi  plays out better in the book in his  chapters you see his admiration for her  and you can sense that it's leading  somewhere he also reflects on how he's  relied on her resourcefulness and  expertise when they were on the can't he  continues to do that when they get their  own ship and he gets to the point where  he actually feels bad about it as far as  their attraction she lays out her  feelings up front and it feels right  when they get together as i mentioned  you do get to know miller better and i  do think it improves his story i thought  that his obsession with julie made more  sense on an emotional level in the show  he spends a lot more time looking into  her so it doesn't seem out of left field  when it consumes him but i found his  trajectory in the book more relatable  parts of his journey are a lot more  clear the way dawes belittles him saying  that he can't even find out who stole  their riot gear comes to mind and  generally i do like how mysterious dawes  was in the book compared to the show  although i have to say i wouldn't want  to give up the jared harris scenes  either he does a great job with the role  and i think it works pretty well on the  show but he did seem more mysterious and  the opa in general seem more mysterious  in the book there miller feels a little  farther gone to me and there's a moment  when he realizes that his boss and his  co-workers all look at him like a washed  up hack he internalizes that and  generally accepts that they're right but  you see through his actions of being a  pretty good detective and figuring  things out that he's not entirely right  and there's something that really works  to close out his story in that book  miller went to eros never intending to  come back he's basically suicidal at  that point and he's okay with going  there and ending his life  and those are the main things for me as  mentioned i watched the show first so i  went into the book with the characters  and events already in my head and it was  fascinating to think about how they  adapted the story for the screen some  changes work better than others but they  all mostly make sense holden and miller  are the perfect povs to start out with  the way that they see things the way  that they experience things it's really  a nice setup for when their stories come  together i also like that the book opens  and closes with two earthers that are  fully on board with supporting the opa  getting into the heads of julie and fred  and their povs is a good way to open and  close things i think the first season is  good but i think there were a lot of  difficulties baked in as far as  presenting the world building it took a  while for me to get into it initially i  wasn't sold on the series until i really  started digging the characters and  thinking of the crew in that found  family kind of way there was a lot to  keep track of on the first time through  and on re-watch so many more of the  details pop out when you already know  what's going to happen and i think  reading the book gives the same kind of  experience i highly recommend it and i  think that's a good place to leave  things let me know in the comments how  you compare the books to the show what  changes did you like which ones didn't  you like and anything else that jumped  out at you i'll be rereading them all  before leviathan falls comes out so let  me know if you want to see more videos  please like this video if you enjoyed it  please subscribe to my channel if you  haven't already and thanks for watching  i'll talk to you soon

